            content: `### 6.1. Ionic Bonding & 6.2. Covalent Bonding
- **Ionic Bonding**: Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions formed by electron transfer (typically metal + nonmetal). Ionic solids have **high melting and boiling points**, are hard and brittle, do not conduct electricity as solids, but **conduct electricity when molten or dissolved in water**.
- **Covalent Bonding**: Attractive force between atoms resulting from the **mutual sharing of one or more pairs of electrons** (Single bond $= 1$ pair, Double bond $= 2$ pairs, Triple bond $= 3$ pairs).
  - **Bond Order, Bond Length & Bond Energy**:
    - **Bond Length**: $\\text{Single bond} > \\text{Double bond} > \\text{Triple bond}$.
    - **Bond Strength (Dissociation Energy)**: $\\text{Triple bond} > \\text{Double bond} > \\text{Single bond}$.
- **Electronegativity ($\\chi$)**: The ability of an atom in a molecule to **attract shared electrons toward itself**.
  - **Periodic Trend**: Increases from left to right across a period and decreases down a group (**Fluorine is the most electronegative element, $\\chi = 4.0$**, followed by $\\text{O} = 3.5$, $\\text{N} \\approx \\text{Cl} = 3.0$).
  - **Classifying Bonds by Electronegativity Difference ($\\Delta \\chi$)**:
    - $\\Delta \\chi < 0.4$: **Pure (Nonpolar) Covalent** (e.g., $\\text{Cl}_2, \\text{C–H}$).
    - $0.4 \\le \\Delta \\chi \\le 1.8$: **Polar Covalent** (partial charges $\\delta^+$ and $\\delta^-$, e.g., $\\text{H–Cl}, \\text{O–H}$).
    - $\\Delta \\chi > 1.8$: **Ionic** (metal + nonmetal, e.g., $\\text{NaCl}$).

---

### 6.3. Lewis Symbols and Structures
- **The Octet Rule**: Main-group atoms tend to gain, lose, or share electrons until they are surrounded by **eight valence electrons** (duet of $2\\;e^-$ for Hydrogen).
- **Three Exceptions to the Octet Rule**:
  1. **Odd-Electron Molecules (Free Radicals)**: Contain an odd total number of valence electrons, leaving one unpaired electron (e.g., $\\text{NO}$ with $11\\;e^-$, $\\text{NO}_2$ with $17\\;e^-$, $\\text{ClO}_2$).
  2. **Electron-Deficient Molecules**: Central atom has fewer than 8 valence electrons (typically Group 2 $\\text{Be}$ with $4\\;e^-$ as in $\\text{BeCl}_2$, and Group 13 $\\text{B/Al}$ with $6\\;e^-$ as in $\\text{BF}_3, \\text{BH}_3$).
  3. **Hypervalent Molecules (Expanded Valence Shells)**: Central atom from **Period 3 or higher ($n \\ge 3$)** has more than 8 valence electrons ($10$ or $12\\;e^-$) due to larger size and accessible $d$ orbitals (e.g., $\\text{PF}_5, \\text{SF}_4, \\text{ClF}_3, \\text{XeF}_2$ have $10\\;e^-$; $\\text{SF}_6, \\text{BrF}_5, \\text{XeF}_4, \\text{ICl}_4^-$ have $12\\;e^-$). *(Period 2 elements $\\text{C, N, O, F}$ can **NEVER** expand their octet beyond $8\\;e^-$!)*

---

### 6.4. Formal Charges and Resonance
- **Formal Charge ($\\text{FC}$)**: The hypothetical charge an atom would have if all bonding electrons were shared equally:
  $$\\text{FC} = (\\text{Valence } e^-) - (\\text{Nonbonding/Lone-pair } e^-) - \\frac{1}{2}(\\text{Bonding } e^-)$$
  - **Rules for Best Lewis Structure**:
    1. All formal charges as close to **$0$** as possible.
    2. Any negative formal charge must reside on the **most electronegative atom**.
    3. Sum of all formal charges must equal $0$ for a neutral molecule or the net charge of a polyatomic ion.
- **Resonance**: When two or more valid Lewis structures with the same atomic arrangement can be written for a molecule/ion (e.g., $\\text{O}_3, \\text{NO}_2^-, \\text{NO}_3^-, \\text{CO}_3^{2-}, \\text{SO}_2, \\text{C}_6\\text{H}_6$), the true structure is a **resonance hybrid** (average) with delocalized $\\pi$ electrons and identical intermediate bond lengths.

---

### 6.5. Strengths of Ionic and Covalent Bonds
- **Covalent Bond Energy & Reaction Enthalpy ($\\Delta H_{\\text{rxn}}$)**:
  - Breaking bonds is **always endothermic ($+$)**; forming bonds is **always exothermic ($-$)**:
    $$\\Delta H_{\\text{rxn}} \\approx \\sum D_{\\text{bonds broken (reactants)}} - \\sum D_{\\text{bonds formed (products)}}$$
- **Ionic Bond Strength, Lattice Energy ($U$) & The Born-Haber Cycle**:
  - **Lattice Energy ($U$)**: Energy required to completely separate $1\\text{ mole}$ of a solid ionic compound into its gaseous ions:
    $$U = \\frac{C \\cdot |Q_1 Q_2|}{r_0}$$
    - Lattice energy increases dramatically with **higher ionic charges ($|Q_1 Q_2|$)** and **smaller interionic distance ($r_0$)** (e.g., $\\text{MgO}$ with $+2/-2$ has a much larger lattice energy and melting point than $\\text{NaCl}$ with $+1/-1$).
  - **Born-Haber Cycle**: Application of **Hess's Law** to calculate lattice energy indirectly from enthalpy of formation ($\\Delta H_f^\\circ$), sublimation enthalpy, bond dissociation energy, ionization energy ($IE$), and electron affinity ($EA$).`
