            content: `### Learning Objectives
Define biomolecules, describe organic and inorganic molecules, identify basic structures, explain precursors, and state properties of water.

### 2.1. Carbohydrates
- **Composition**: Composed of Carbon ($\\text{C}$), Hydrogen ($\\text{H}$), and Oxygen ($\\text{O}$), typically in a $1:2:1$ ratio ($\\text{C}_n(\\text{H}_2\\text{O})_n$).
- **Three Major Classes**:
  1. **Monosaccharides (Simple Sugars)**: Monomers linked by hydroxyl groups and a carbonyl group (aldose or ketose).
     - *Pentoses ($5\\text{C}$)*: **Ribose** ($\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{10}\\text{O}_5$, in RNA/ATP) and **Deoxyribose** ($\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{10}\\text{O}_4$, lacking oxygen at $\\text{C}_2$, in DNA).
     - *Hexoses ($6\\text{C}$, $\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6$ isomers)*: **Glucose** (aldose, primary cellular fuel), **Galactose** (aldose, milk sugar component), **Fructose** (ketose, fruit sugar).
  2. **Disaccharides**: Formed by condensation (dehydration synthesis) of two monosaccharides via a **glycosidic linkage** (releasing $1\\;\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$):
     - **Maltose** (Malt sugar) $= \\text{Glucose} + \\text{Glucose}$ ($\\alpha\\text{-}1,4$ glycosidic bond; reducing sugar).
     - **Lactose** (Milk sugar) $= \\text{Glucose} + \\text{Galactose}$ ($\\beta\\text{-}1,4$ bond; reducing sugar).
     - **Sucrose** (Table/Cane sugar) $= \\text{Glucose} + \\text{Fructose}$ ($\\alpha\\text{-}1,2$ bond; **non-reducing sugar**).
  3. **Polysaccharides**: Complex polymers of monosaccharides:
     - **Starch** (Plant energy storage: unbranched helical *amylose* + branched *amylopectin*, $\\alpha$-glucose).
     - **Glycogen** (Animal energy storage in liver and skeletal muscle; highly branched $\\alpha$-glucose).
     - **Cellulose** (Plant cell wall structural polymer of $\\beta$-glucose linked by $\\beta\\text{-}1,4$ glycosidic bonds; indigestible by humans lacking cellulase).

### 2.2. Lipids
- **Types**: Fats, oils, waxes, phospholipids, and steroids (cholesterol, sex hormones).
- **Triglycerides**: Formed by ester linkages between **1 Glycerol** molecule and **3 Fatty Acids**:
  - **Saturated Fatty Acids**: No $\\text{C}=\\text{C}$ double bonds; straight chains packed tightly; solid at room temperature (animal fats).
  - **Unsaturated & Polyunsaturated Fatty Acids**: Contain one or more *cis* $\\text{C}=\\text{C}$ double bonds causing kinks; liquid at room temperature (plant oils).
- **Properties & Biological Importance**: Hydrophobic (insoluble in water, soluble in non-polar solvents); form **phospholipid bilayers** of cell membranes; yield $>2\\times$ more ATP per gram than carbohydrates; provide thermal insulation, mechanical organ protection, waterproofing (waxes), and steroid hormone signaling.

### 2.3. Proteins
- **Monomers**: **Amino Acids** composed of $\\text{C, H, O, N}$ (and sometimes Sulfur $\\text{S}$ in cysteine and methionine).
- **Amino Acid Structure**: Central $\\alpha$-carbon bonded to an **Amino group ($-\\text{NH}_2$)**, a **Carboxyl group ($-\\text{COOH}$)**, a Hydrogen atom, and a **variable side chain ($\\text{R}$ group)**.
- **Peptide Bonds**: Covalent $\\text{C}-\\text{N}$ bond formed between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amino group of the next via dehydration synthesis.
- **Four Levels of Protein Structure**:
  1. **Primary ($1^\\circ$)**: Linear sequence of amino acids determined by DNA gene sequence.
  2. **Secondary ($2^\\circ$)**: Local folding into **$\\alpha$-helices** and **$\\beta$-pleated sheets** stabilized by hydrogen bonds along the polypeptide backbone.
  3. **Tertiary ($3^\\circ$)**: Overall 3D folding of a single polypeptide stabilized by R-group interactions (disulfide bridges, ionic bonds, hydrophobic interactions, H-bonds).
  4. **Quaternary ($4^\\circ$)**: Assembly of two or more polypeptide subunits (e.g., **Hemoglobin** with $2\\alpha$ and $2\\beta$ chains; collagen).
- **Functions**: Enzymatic catalysis, structural support (keratin, collagen), transport (hemoglobin, membrane channels), defense (antibodies), hormones (insulin), and growth control.`
