            content: `### 4.1 The "Restoration" of the "Solomonic" Dynasty (1270)
- In **1270**, **Yikuno-Amlak** (r. 1270–1285), a ruler from Amhara supported by Tigrayan and Shewan clergy, defeated the last Zagwe king **Yetbarak**.
- **Ideological Legitimation (*Kibre Negest* — "The Glory of Kings")**:
  - Translated from Arabic into Ge'ez in the early 14th century by **Nebure-Id Yishaq of Aksum**.
  - Narrates the union of **King Solomon of Israel** and **Queen Makeda (the Queen of Sheba)**, the birth of **Menilek I (*Ibn al-Hakim*)**, and the bringing of the **Ark of the Covenant (*Tabot*)** to Aksum.
  - Declared that only descendants of the "Solomonic" line had the divine right to rule Ethiopia, branding the Zagwe dynasty as illegitimate usurpers.

### 4.2 Power Struggle, Consolidation, Territorial Expansion and Religious Processes
- **4.2.1 Succession Problem and the 'Royal Prison' of Amba Gishen**:
  - Upon the death of **Yagba-Tsion** (1294), violent succession wars broke out among his sons.
  - Around **1300**, **King Widim-Ra'ad** established the **'Royal Prison' of Amba Gishen** in southern Wollo: all male royal descendants (except the reigning king and his immediate heirs) were confined on the flat-topped mountain guarded by loyal soldiers until a new successor was summoned—successfully preventing civil wars for over two centuries until Amba Gishen was destroyed by **Imam Ahmed Gragn in 1540**.
- **4.2.2 Consolidation and Territorial Expansion**:
  - **Mobile Royal Capitals**: Unlike Aksum or Roha, medieval Solomonic emperors ruled from **roving military camps (*Ketema*)** moving across Menz, Tegulet, Yerer, and Zequala to administer vast territories and secure firewood/provisions.
  - **Emperor Amde-Tsion (r. 1314–1344)**:
    - The most expansionist medieval Christian monarch; transformed the kingdom into an empire.
    - Subdued **Damot, Hadiya, Gojjam, Bizamo, Fatagar, Dawaro, Bali**, and defeated a coalition of Muslim Sultanates led by **Sabr ad-Din I of Ifat** in **1332**, extending dominion from the Red Sea coast to the southern lakes.
  - **Emperor Zara-Yaqob (r. 1434–1468)**:
    - Consolidated administrative centralization, founded the semi-permanent capital of **Debre Birhan** (1454), defeated Sultan **Ahmad Badlay of Adal** at the **Battle of Gomit (1445)**, and authored religious treatises (*Metsahafe Birhan*, *Metsahafe Milad*).
- **4.2.3 Evangelization, Religious Movements, and Reforms**:
  - Monastic evangelists like **Iyesus-Mo'a** (Monastery of **Hayq Estifanos**) and **Abune Tekle-Haymanot** (Monastery of **Debre Asbo / Debre Libanos**) spearheaded evangelization in Shewa, Damot, and Gojjam.
  - **The Ewostatewos Movement**: Led by **Abba Ewostatewos** (14th century), who advocated the strict observance of **both the Saturday (Jewish/Old Testament) Sabbath and the Sunday Sabbath**. Persecuted by the Egyptian *Abun* and royal court, he traveled to Egypt, Jerusalem, and died in Armenia; his followers (*Ewostatians*) established northern monasteries like **Debre Bizen**.
  - **The Estifanosites (*Deqiqe Estifanos* — Stephanites)**: Founded by **Abba Estifanos of Gundagunde** (15th century); championed strict monastic poverty, **rejected bowing (*Prostration / Sigdet*) before the Emperor**, and opposed Zara-Yaqob's Marian cult excesses, suffering severe persecution.
  - **Religious Reforms of Emperor Zara-Yaqob**: At the **Council of Debre Mitmaq (1450)**, Zara-Yaqob reconciled the **Ewostatians** by officially legalizing the **dual Sabbath (Saturday and Sunday)**, mandated the wearing of the Christian neck-cord (*Mateb*), and instituted 33 annual feasts of St. Mary.`
