            heading: '7.1 Post-1941 Imperial Period (Restoration, External Relations, Socio-Economy & Oppositions)',
            content: `### 7.1 Post-1941 Imperial Period
- **7.1.1 Restoration and Consolidation of Imperial Power and External Relations**:
  - **British Era (1941–1952)**:
    - Following the 1941 liberation, Britain treated Ethiopia as Occupied Enemy Territory (*OETA*).
    - **First Anglo-Ethiopian Agreement (Jan 31, 1942)**: Recognized Ethiopia's sovereignty on paper, but gave the British envoy precedence, placed the Ethiopian army and police under British control, and kept the **Ogaden, Haud, Reserved Area (bordering Somaliland), and the Ethio-Djibouti Railway** under direct British military administration.
    - **Second Anglo-Ethiopian Agreement (Dec 1944)**: Restored control over the railway and army to Ethiopia, though Britain did not fully evacuate the **Ogaden until 1948** and the **Haud & Reserved Area until 1954**.
  - **Shift to the United States (1950s–1974)**:
    - Seeking to break British dominance, Emperor Haile-Selassie forged a strategic alliance with the **USA** (founded **Ethiopian Airlines in 1945/46** with TWA, Point Four Agreement in 1952).
    - **1953 Ethio-US Defense & Mutual Assistance Agreements**: Granted the US a 25-year lease on the **Kagnew Communications Station** in Asmara (a vital Cold War global radio-intelligence base), in exchange for over **$200 million in US military aid and training** (making Ethiopia the largest recipient of US military aid in Sub-Saharan Africa).
  - **Federation and Union of Eritrea (1952–1962)**:
    - After WWII, the **UN General Assembly Resolution 390-A(V) (Dec 2, 1950)** federated **Eritrea** (an autonomous unit with its own parliament, flag, and languages—Tigrigna and Arabic) under the Ethiopian Crown, effective **September 1952**.
    - However, the Imperial government systematically eroded Eritrean autonomy, culminating on **November 14, 1962** in the dissolution of the federation and reduction of Eritrea to Ethiopia's **14th province**, which fueled the 30-year Eritrean armed struggle (**ELF** founded in 1960/61 by **Hamid Idris Awate**, later **EPLF** in the 1970s).
- **7.1.2 Socio-Economic Developments & Consolidation of Autocracy**:
  - **1955 Revised Constitution**: Promulgated on the Emperor's Silver Jubilee (Nov 1955) partly to match Eritrea's modern constitution; introduced **universal adult suffrage for the Chamber of Deputies**, but further entrenched **absolute imperial autocracy** by declaring the Emperor's person *"sacred, his dignity inviolable, and his power indisputable."*
  - **Three Five-Year Development Plans (1957–1973)**:
    1. **First Five-Year Plan (1957–1962)**: Focused on **physical infrastructure** (transport, highways, telecommunications, Koka Hydroelectric Dam).
    2. **Second Five-Year Plan (1962–1967)**: Focused on **manufacturing industry** (import-substitution consumer goods: textiles, sugar at Wonji/Metehara, cement) and mining.
    3. **Third Five-Year Plan (1968–1973)**: Focused on **large-scale commercial agriculture** (CADU in Arsi, WADU in Wolayta).
  - **Agrarian Crisis**: Because over 70–80% of southern peasants remained landless **tenants (*Chisegna*)** paying up to **75% (*Erbo, Siso, Ekul*)** of their harvest to absentee landlords without security of tenure, agricultural productivity stagnated.
- **7.1.3 Oppositions and the Downfall of the Monarchical Regime**:
  1. **Early Plots & Conspiracies**: **Blatta Takele Wolde-Hawaryat** (republican patriot who plotted repeatedly until killed in a shootout in 1969), **Dejazmach Belay Zeleke** (hanged in 1945), and **Bitwoded Negash Bezabih** (1951 plot).
  2. **The December 1960 Attempted Coup d'État**:
     - Led by **Brigadier General Mengistu Neway** (Commander of the Imperial Bodyguard) and his Columbia-educated brother **Germame Neway** (alongside Police Chief **Workneh Gebeyehu** and Security Chief **Tsege Dibu**) while the Emperor was visiting Brazil.
     - Proclaimed Crown Prince Asfa Wossen a constitutional monarch and exposed Ethiopia's backwardness compared to newly independent African nations. Though crushed by the Army and Air Force (Germame died, Mengistu was hanged in March 1961), the 1960 coup **shattered the myth of imperial invincibility** and inspired the student movement.
  3. **Major Peasant Rebellions**:
     - **First Woyane Rebellion in Tigray (1943)**: Led by **Blatta Haile-Mariam Redda** against corrupt administrative centralization, heavy taxation, and British/imperial troops; crushed in October 1943 with **British Royal Air Force (RAF) aerial bombardment** of Mekelle and Corbetta.
     - **Yejju Peasant Rebellions (1948 & 1970)**: Led by **Qegnazmach Melaku Taye** and **Unda Mohammed** against land alienation and mechanized eviction.
     - **Gedeo Peasant Uprising (1960)**: At **Michille**, Gedeo peasants rose against northern landlord exploitation (*Qalad* land seizure) and heavy tenancy tribute.
     - **Bale Peasant Rebellion (1963–1970)**: Longest and most organized rural insurgency, sparked by land alienation, heavy taxation, and religious/ethnic marginalization; led by **General Waqo Gutu** (with Somali support) until pacified in 1970 when the Emperor appointed **General Jagama Kello** governor of Bale and pardoned Waqo Gutu.
     - **Gojjam Peasant Rebellion (1968)**: Triggered by the **1967 Agricultural Income Tax Proclamation** (which peasants feared would destroy their ancestral communal ***Rist*** tenure) and the corrupt rule of Governor **Dejazmach Tsehayu Enqu-Selassie**; forced the Emperor to cancel the tax, cancel arrears, and remove Tsehayu.
  4. **Movements of Nations and Nationalities (Mecha-Tulama Association, 1963–1967)**:
     - Founded in **January 1963** as a self-help development association (building schools, clinics, roads), it grew into a pan-Oromo movement for cultural dignity and socio-political equality under **Brigadier General Tadesse Birru** and **Colonel Alemu Qitessa**, **Haile-Mariam Gemeda**, and **Mamo Mezemir** before being banned in **1966/1967**.
  5. **The Ethiopian Student Movement (ESM, 1960s–1974)**:
     - Spearheaded by university students (USUAA inside Ethiopia; ESUNA in North America; ESUE in Europe), raising the historic slogan ***"Land to the Tiller" (Meret Larashu)*** in **February 1965** and confronting the **"Question of Nationalities"** in **Wallelign Mekonnen's** famous November 1969 article (shortly before student President **Tilahun Gizaw** was assassinated in Dec 1969).`
